{-
   What a qualified type argument refuses - the other half of `Argument.yana`.

   # resolver: ownership

   §2.4 distributes ownership qualification "through logical type structure", and the word doing the
   work is *structure*: the qualifier names positions inside the value, so it is only writable where
   there are positions to name. Each refusal below is one of those conditions, and each has its own
   fix.

   The one condition that is *not* here is the teardown residue, and it is in `Reject.Residue.yana`
   because it cannot share a file: it is checked after `classifyTypes`, which a program with any
   declaration error never reaches.
-}

data Guard {id: I32}

instance Drop(Guard):
    fn drop(->value: Guard) -> {} = {}

data Boxed(a) {value: a}

-- A parameter no field holds. `Tag(a)` is a phantom, so the qualifier describes a position the value
-- does not have - which is the one thing a shape may not claim.
data Tag(a) {n: I32}

fn phantom(g: a'Guard) -> Tag(a'Guard) = Tag {n: 1}

-- A parameter held where no projection reaches it. A path is a list of field steps, so an element of
-- an array has no static position for the qualifier to sit at.
data Row(a) {items: [a *4]}

fn inArray(g: a'Guard) -> Row(a'Guard) = Row {items: [g, g, g, g]}

-- One record deeper. The position is reachable, and laying it out would mean laying `Boxed` out
-- differently too - a manifestation keyed on a shape rather than on a type.
data Nest(a) {inner: Boxed(a)}

fn nested(g: a'Guard) -> Nest(a'Guard) = Nest {inner: Boxed {value: g}}

-- The same bound reached from the other side: an argument that qualifies a position of its own. The
-- position `Maybe` would hold is `[.Just, .value]`, a second *field* step - where `Argument.yana`'s
-- `Maybe(%s'U8)` composes, because a pointee step is an indirection and not a place in the bytes.
fn deep(g: a'Guard) -> Maybe(Boxed(a'Guard)) = Just(Boxed {value: g})

-- A result that borrows and does not say from where, one constructor in. The unqualified `&` is
-- refused inside a type argument for the reason it is refused at a root: nothing at a use site can
-- work out whose storage it is.
fn anonymous(g: &Guard) -> Maybe(&Guard) = Just(g)

-- An alias stands for a type reached through its own name, so there is nothing here to qualify.
alias Optional(a) = Maybe(a)

fn aliased(g: a'Guard) -> Optional(a'Guard) = Just(g)

-- A vector's lanes are a register and an atomic's content is reached only by its own operations, so
-- neither has an interior position for a qualifier to name.
fn lanes(v: a'I32) -> Vec(a'I32, 4) = splat(0)

{-
   And an **instance head**, where a qualifier is not a restriction but a category mistake.

   An instance is selected by a logical type, and a capability is not one: `'[a]` names `Array(a)`
   reached through a shared window, which is the same handle `[a]` and `Array(a)` intern to. So this
   head and the unqualified one are the same instance written twice.

   It is the one refusal here that a library written against the *shipping* stage runs into, and it
   is a decision rather than a gap - `type.def` has no slice sibling, where the old stage's `Flat(a)`
   is a type of its own and makes the two heads two.

   The member's subject is `[a]`, because the class's `self: c` at `c = Array(a)` is the view - a
   shared variable position bound to a type with a view *is* the view, which is the one form a body
   compiled once can take - and an instance member's forms have to be the class member's.
-}
class Holds(c):
    fn holds(self: c) -> I32

instance Holds(Array(a)):
    fn holds(self: [a]) -> I32 = 1

instance Holds('[a]):
    fn holds(self: [a]) -> I32 = 2

-- And a field, which cannot qualify one at all: the positions are found by walking the applied
-- declaration's fields, and a field's own type is resolved inside the pass that reads them.
data Holder {maybe: Maybe(a'Guard)}

fn main() -> I32 = 0
